\pard\s11\widctlpar \keepn\sa240\qc\b0\i\fs24 Styles, style sets and paragraph settings in AhkWord\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\i0 This document is a tutorial, and also an example: every paragraph in it has a style, and the settings it describes are used in it. Put the cursor in any paragraph and the Style box on the Home tab shows its style. Open Home > Paragraph > Paragraph settings (the \'b6 button) to see its indents, spacing and page-break settings.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 1. What a style is\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 A style is a named set of formatting for a whole paragraph: its font, size, bold, italic, underline and colour, and its paragraph settings (alignment, indents, space before and after, line spacing, and how it behaves at a page break). Headings also have a level, 1 to 6, which the outline, Markdown and HTML use.\par\s0\widctlpar 
Formatting with styles instead of by hand has two advantages. Every paragraph with the same style looks the same, and when you change a style, every paragraph that has it changes with it. A twenty-page brief with forty headings needs one change, not forty.\par\s0\widctlpar 
Every document has these styles: Normal (body text), Title, Subtitle, Heading 1 to Heading 6, Quote and Code. You can add your own. This document has two of its own, Step and Tip.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 2. Giving a paragraph a style\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 A style always applies to whole paragraphs. You don't need to select a paragraph first: put the cursor anywhere in it. To style several paragraphs, select across them.\par

\pard\s14\widctlpar \keep\fi-360\li360\sa80\tx360 1.\tab Click in the paragraph.\par\s14\widctlpar 
2.\tab Choose a style in the Style box (Home > Styles), or use a shortcut: Ctrl+0 Normal, Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+6 Heading 1 to Heading 6, Ctrl+7 Code.\par\s14\widctlpar 
3.\tab Press Enter at the end of a heading, Title or Subtitle: the new paragraph is Normal. Each style has a "style after Enter", set in Modify Style.\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \li360\ri360\sb120\sa120\i Tip: formatting you give part of a paragraph, such as a bold word, stays when you change the paragraph's style. Formatting that covers more than half the paragraph, such as a line pasted from elsewhere, gives way to the new style, as in Word.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b\i0 Clear Formatting\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 Ctrl+Space (or Styles > Clear Formatting) puts selected text back to exactly what its style says. With nothing selected, it does that to the whole paragraph the cursor is in, its indents and spacing included. One Ctrl+Z undoes it.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b Copy and paste\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 Copying and pasting keeps each paragraph's style, also between two AhkWord windows. A style the other document doesn't have comes along with its definition.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 3. Style sets\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 A style set is a whole family of styles that suit each other. Choosing one changes every style in the document at once, so it changes the look of the whole document, not of one paragraph. Three sets come with AhkWord, and none of them is Word's:\par

\pard\s14\widctlpar \keep\fi-1440\li1440\sa80\tx1440 Classic\tab Times New Roman 12 with black headings, for court papers, contracts and letters. This is the default, and this document uses it.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Plain\tab Segoe UI 11 with bold headings: the editor's look before it had styles.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Modern\tab Georgia text with Segoe UI headings in dark grey, and a little more room between lines.\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120 Choose a set in the Styles menu (Home > Styles > Styles). The menu's label always names the set the document uses, for example "Styles: Classic". Once you change one of its styles, it reads "Styles: Classic (changed)". Styles of your own aren't part of a set, so they stay as they are when you change sets.\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \li360\ri360\sb120\sa120\i Try it: choose Modern, then Plain, then Classic again. The words don't move; only the look changes.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\i0\fs28 4. Changing a style\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 The Styles menu has everything for changing styles:\par

\pard\s14\widctlpar \keep\fi-2520\li2520\sa80\tx2520 Modify Style\tab changes the style of the paragraph the cursor is in: font, size, colour, alignment, indents, spacing, line spacing, heading level and the style after Enter. Its Paragraph button opens the full Paragraph dialog for the style.\par\s14\widctlpar 
New Style\tab makes a style from the paragraph the cursor is in, and gives it to that paragraph. Format one paragraph the way you want it, then make it a style.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Update Style to Match\tab does the reverse: the paragraph's style takes the paragraph's look, and every other paragraph with the style follows.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Delete Style\tab removes a style of your own; its paragraphs become Normal. The built-in styles can't be deleted.\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120 This document's Normal style has been changed in this way: it has 6 points after each paragraph, which Classic's Normal hasn't. That's why its Styles menu reads "Styles: Classic (changed)".\par\s0\widctlpar 
A change affects this document only. Save Styles as My Default (in the same menu) gives every new document this document's styles, your changes included.\par\s0\widctlpar 
A document you open keeps its own look. Its styles are taken from the document itself, so opening a file from someone else never reformats it.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 5. Paragraph settings\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 The Paragraph dialog sets everything about the selected paragraphs, the way Word's does. Open it with the \'b6 button in Home > Paragraph, or with Line Spacing > Line Spacing Options. It has two tabs and a preview that shows the result as you go.\par\s0\widctlpar 
If the selected paragraphs differ in a setting, that box is empty. Leave it empty and each paragraph keeps its own; type a value and they all get it. OK makes all the changes in one step, so one Ctrl+Z undoes them.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b Indents and Spacing\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 Alignment, the left and right indents, and a special indent for the first line: First line indents it, Hanging pulls it out to the left (as in the numbered steps above). Then the space before and after the paragraph, and the line spacing: Single, 1.5 lines, Double, At least or Exactly so many points, or Multiple (such as 1.15).\par\s0\widctlpar 
Indents are shown in inches and spacing in points, but you can type any unit: 1.27 cm, 36pt, 0.5in or 0.5".\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \fi720\li720\ri360\sb120\sa120\i This paragraph's own settings: a left indent of 0.5", and a first line indented another 0.5". Click in it and open the Paragraph dialog to see them.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b\i0 Line and Page Breaks\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 These settings decide where a page may end. They show when you print, and in Print Preview, Export as PDF and View > Multiple Pages.\par

\pard\s14\widctlpar \keep\fi-2880\li2880\sa80\tx2880 Widow/Orphan control\tab never leaves a single line of a paragraph alone at the foot of a page or the top of the next. Normal has it on.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Keep with next\tab keeps the paragraph on the same page as the next one. All the headings in this document have it, so a heading is never stranded at the bottom of a page.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Keep lines together\tab moves the whole paragraph to the next page rather than splitting it.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Page break before\tab starts the paragraph on a new page, without a page break of its own to delete later.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Suppress line numbers\tab leaves the paragraph out of Layout > Line Numbers.\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120 When a rule can't be kept without leaving a page empty, it is set aside, as in Word. For example, every paragraph on a page could have Keep with next.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b Tabs\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 The Tabs button in the Paragraph dialog sets the paragraphs' tab stops: a position, an alignment (left, center, right, decimal or bar) and a leader (dots, dashes or a line). The lines below use a right-aligned tab stop at 6 inches with a dot leader, as a table of contents does. The dots show on screen and in print, and Word and LibreOffice files keep them. HTML has no tab stops, so a browser shows the line with a plain gap; AhkWord keeps the tab stops in the HTML for itself, so the dots come back when you open the file in AhkWord again. The same dialog sets the default tab stops for the whole document.\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \tqr\tldot\tx8640 What a style is\tab 1\par\s0\widctlpar 
Giving a paragraph a style\tab 1\par\s0\widctlpar 
Style sets\tab 2\par\s0\widctlpar 
Paragraph settings\tab 2\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b Set As Default\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 Set As Default in the Paragraph dialog gives the paragraph's style the settings the dialog shows. You choose whether that's for this document only, or for new documents too. Every paragraph with the style changes, as when you modify the style.\par

\pard\s2\widctlpar \keepn\s2\sb240\sa120\b Settings only a style has\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0 In Modify Style > Paragraph there is one more setting: Don't add space between paragraphs of the same style. With it, a run of paragraphs in that style has no space between them, only before the first and after the last. Use it for a list of addresses or a block of short lines. The Tip style below has it.\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \li360\ri360\sb120\i First line of a block in the Tip style.\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \li360\ri360 Second line: no space before it, though Tip has space before and after.\par

\pard\s15\widctlpar \li360\ri360\sa120 Third line, and then the usual space after the block.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\i0\fs28 6. Styles in other formats\par

\pard\s14\widctlpar \keep\fi-2160\li2160\sa80\tx2160\b0\fs24 RTF\tab AhkWord's own format keeps everything: the styles, their definitions, and every paragraph setting.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Word, LibreOffice\tab Files saved through Word or LibreOffice have real styles (Title, Subtitle, Heading 1 to 6, Quote, Code and your own) with the same look, and the line and page break settings.\par\s14\widctlpar 
HTML\tab Headings become h1 to h6, other styles are named in the page, and each styled paragraph carries its look, so browsers show it as AhkWord does. Opened again in AhkWord, the styles come back.\par\s14\widctlpar 
Markdown\tab keeps headings (#) and code; other styles become plain paragraphs, and Markdown has no spacing or indents.\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 7. For scripts\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \sa120\b0\fs24 Everything here can be done from an AutoHotkey script (Tools > Automation API lists it all). For example:\par

\pard\s13\widctlpar \f1\fs20 Rte.Style := "Heading 1"\par\s13\widctlpar 
Rte.StyleSet := "Modern"\par\s13\widctlpar 
Rte.ModifyStyle("Normal", "Font=Georgia`nSize=12`nAfter=6")\par\s13\widctlpar 
Rte.SetParagraphFormat("LeftIndent=0.5in; FirstLine=-0.5in; KeepTogether=1")\par\s13\widctlpar 
Rte.SetTabs("6in/right/dots")\par\s13\widctlpar 
MsgBox Rte.ParagraphFormat()\par

\pard\s1\widctlpar \keepn\s1\sb240\sa120\b\f0\fs28 8. Not there yet\par

\pard\s0\widctlpar \keepn\sa120\b0\fs24 Character styles (a named look for words rather than paragraphs) and numbered styles, whose numbering survives cut and paste, are planned. Mirror indents and hyphenation aren't supported. Dragging text with the mouse keeps only the heading styles; cut and paste keeps them all.\par

\pard\s12\widctlpar \li720\ri720\sa240 "Format the style, not the paragraph." Change a style once and the whole document follows.\par
